You messed with John Hopkins University.

00:32:25.650 --> 00:32:27.990
So you know those plans you've always had for the Minifield Library?

00:32:28.190 --> 00:32:29.670
Well, just kiss those goodbye, okay?

00:32:29.790 --> 00:32:29.970
Because they're...

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:31.580
Their reach goes way beyond Oklahoma.

00:32:31.840 --> 00:32:34.160
This is a new low even for you, Fleischman.

00:32:34.300 --> 00:32:36.000
Wait a minute. You vandalized my property.

00:32:36.120 --> 00:32:37.100
You expect me to feel guilty?

00:32:37.570 --> 00:32:41.220
You have the unmitigated gall to slip me a placebo

00:32:41.220 --> 00:32:44.040
and then sit back and watch me like I'm some mindless gerbil

00:32:44.040 --> 00:32:47.420
running around in its cage, convinced he's crossing the Rubicon.

00:32:47.660 --> 00:32:48.380
Believe this.

00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:51.980
Vigilance. That is the key word in boot camp from day one.

00:32:52.000 --> 00:32:54.360
Well, I let my guard down for a friend.

00:32:54.360 --> 00:32:56.720
I turned off my radar in the heat of battle.

00:32:57.170 --> 00:32:59.070
Well, let me tell you something, pal of mine.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:03.320
Try and make me a footnote in the New England Journal of Medicine.

00:33:03.660 --> 00:33:08.140
I'll have my litigators crawling all over you like a hive of killer bees.

00:33:17.010 --> 00:33:19.320
Chris, I gotta get something off my chest.

00:33:19.730 --> 00:33:20.190
What's up?

00:33:21.100 --> 00:33:21.660
I don't know.

00:33:22.490 --> 00:33:24.030
Probably really shouldn't even bother you.

00:33:29.460 --> 00:33:29.960
Maybe you'll...

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:30.820
you'd rather tell me.

00:33:31.880 --> 00:33:34.280
Great, because to tell you the truth,

00:33:34.420 --> 00:33:35.680
I was getting kind of afraid to ask.

00:33:36.140 --> 00:33:37.120
It's about time.

00:33:37.580 --> 00:33:38.700
You know how helpless it feels

00:33:38.700 --> 00:33:40.360
watching you boot routine grounders?

00:33:40.970 --> 00:33:42.390
Hey, I got you on my knee, don't I?

00:33:42.870 --> 00:33:43.930
Maybe I should come back.

00:33:44.380 --> 00:33:46.380
No, please, Ed, I really want to help.

00:33:46.700 --> 00:33:48.890
Oh, great, because I really need help.

00:33:49.510 --> 00:33:50.470
I did something horrible.

00:33:51.440 --> 00:33:53.120
I got Dr. Fleischman's medication

00:33:53.120 --> 00:33:55.200
all mixed up with the placebo

00:33:55.200 --> 00:33:56.880
so nobody knows who's taking what.

00:33:57.840 --> 00:33:58.800
That's a biggie, Ed.

00:33:58.960 --> 00:33:59.680
What were you thinking?

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:02.580
Well, I panicked. I thought if no one found out...

00:34:02.740 --> 00:34:03.920
Cat's out of the bag now.

00:34:04.100 --> 00:34:05.240
Yeah, really screwed up, huh?

00:34:05.870 --> 00:34:06.570
Big time.

00:34:07.360 --> 00:34:09.160
Hey, let's not be so hard on Ed.

00:34:09.300 --> 00:34:10.930
I mean, you know, what'd you screw up?

00:34:10.990 --> 00:34:12.710
The first leg of a million-mile relay?

00:34:13.140 --> 00:34:14.600
Aren't we being tested all the time?

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:18.120
Maybe the real test is seeing how we deal with an experiment gone bad, huh?

00:34:18.700 --> 00:34:19.800
There you go again.

00:34:20.680 --> 00:34:24.100
I'm just saying, the act of analyzing data changes it.

00:34:24.240 --> 00:34:26.460
I mean, if Heisenberg was uncertain, why are you so sure?

00:34:26.800 --> 00:34:27.900
You better leave this to me.

00:34:28.810 --> 00:34:29.960
Ed, you can't...

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:31.940
You can't have your cake and eat it, too, can you?

00:34:32.260 --> 00:34:33.280
I don't want any cake.

00:34:33.640 --> 00:34:36.840
You're not a kid. You're 24 and a shaman in the rough.

00:34:37.610 --> 00:34:40.440
You gotta face the music. Bite the bullet. Take your lumps.

00:34:40.830 --> 00:34:42.430
Well, that sounds a little glib, you saw.

00:34:42.940 --> 00:34:45.200
Who are you gonna listen to, me or the dummy?

00:34:47.790 --> 00:34:49.330
I guess I should tell Dr. Fleischmann.

00:34:49.650 --> 00:34:51.630
Of course you gotta tell Dr. Fleischmann.

00:34:52.480 --> 00:34:53.740
I wonder when it would be a good time.

00:34:54.240 --> 00:34:55.500
No time like the present.

00:34:56.350 --> 00:34:59.740
Right. Well, thanks, Chris. I need to hear that.

00:35:00.860 --> 00:35:01.720
No problem, Ed.

00:35:21.280 --> 00:35:21.640
Esau?

00:35:27.780 --> 00:35:28.100
Whoa!

00:35:28.620 --> 00:35:29.400
Have you seen Esau?

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:32.330
Esau? You didn't lose a little guy, did you?

00:35:37.550 --> 00:35:38.150
Warm you up.

00:35:40.620 --> 00:35:42.520
Pauline, have you seen anybody in here with Esau?

00:35:43.540 --> 00:35:44.540
Is that who you're looking for?

00:35:44.760 --> 00:35:46.550
Ruthann, what are you doing?

00:35:47.260 --> 00:35:48.700
I didn't tell you you could take him.

00:35:49.420 --> 00:35:52.620
I just brought him home to sew up his sweater and he snagged.

00:35:53.530 --> 00:35:54.990
We thought we'd surprise you.

00:35:55.750 --> 00:35:56.470
He does look better.

00:35:56.950 --> 00:35:58.110
I got a show to do, you know?

00:35:58.910 --> 00:35:59.970
You wouldn't like it if I bought...

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:02.040
I stopped in your garage and took your truck for a spin

00:36:02.040 --> 00:36:04.780
because I thought the cars needed blown out.

00:36:05.000 --> 00:36:06.580
I'm sorry, all right.

00:36:07.100 --> 00:36:09.000
I guess I should have asked you.

00:36:09.520 --> 00:36:11.440
Yeah, you're right, you should have.

00:36:18.410 --> 00:36:21.010
Pay no attention to that man behind the curtain.

00:36:22.740 --> 00:36:25.150
I'm sorry, I'm a little ballistic.

00:36:25.660 --> 00:36:28.300
That's okay. See you later, Esau.

00:36:29.090 --> 00:36:29.970
Bye, Ruthie.

00:36:34.310 --> 00:36:42.330
Right. There's just two huge problems with the initial reportings.

00:36:42.510 --> 00:36:48.810
A, half the sample group is convinced that the drug has made them human dynamos,

00:36:48.930 --> 00:36:53.580
and B, the placebo is reporting side effects at the same levels as the drug.

00:36:53.780 --> 00:36:57.100
It's literally as if someone had taken the results and just tossed them up in the air.

00:36:57.920 --> 00:36:58.600
Someone did.

00:37:02.880 --> 00:37:09.740
No, no, I'm right here. Actually, I'm gonna call you right back, okay?

00:37:10.720 --> 00:37:16.150
I'm sorry, I will. Yeah. What'd you say?

00:37:16.860 --> 00:37:18.500
Pills are all mixed up, Dr. Fleshman.

00:37:18.800 --> 00:37:19.040
What?

00:37:19.620 --> 00:37:24.670
The other day, when you went to go get Marilyn, Bartholomew, he snuck in and...

00:37:25.140 --> 00:37:28.340
while I chased him around and tables got knocked over.

00:37:30.830 --> 00:37:31.790
Oh, my God.

00:37:32.220 --> 00:37:33.760
Ed, oh, my God.

00:37:34.260 --> 00:37:38.120
Yeah, I tried to get all the pills and put them back together best I could, you know.

00:37:38.850 --> 00:37:40.430
But, of course, they all look the same.

00:37:41.190 --> 00:37:42.390
Guess that's the point, huh?

00:37:43.590 --> 00:37:45.130
I really blew it, Dr. Fleischman.

00:37:45.970 --> 00:37:46.790
That's it. It's over.

00:37:46.950 --> 00:37:48.690
I mean, the clinical trial, it's over.

00:37:48.850 --> 00:37:50.620
I mean, the whole study.

00:37:51.440 --> 00:37:59.960
I mean, I'm going to convince them that every negative impression they ever had about...

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:04.780
me is true boy if he wasn't john hopkins material then he certainly isn't now i can just hear their

00:38:04.780 --> 00:38:17.420
tongues clucking four thousand miles away gonna be okay dr fleishman yeah fine

00:39:45.520 --> 00:39:47.720
He comes up to Buddha and says,

00:39:48.160 --> 00:39:50.040
Is it true that right after you were born,

00:39:50.200 --> 00:39:52.440
you said, I am the center of the universe?

00:39:52.740 --> 00:39:55.000
And Buddha thinks for a minute and says,

00:39:55.400 --> 00:39:56.820
Don't all babies say that?

00:39:58.380 --> 00:39:59.900
Didn't I tell you even something?

00:40:00.520 --> 00:40:04.360
Would you mind, Ruthie? There's a knot in my neck you wouldn't believe.

00:40:04.880 --> 00:40:09.310
That's it. Oh, yeah. Oh, yeah. Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:40:09.530 --> 00:40:13.010
We could use a straight shooter like you in Congress right now.

00:40:13.700 --> 00:40:15.560
What's one more dummy gonna do?

00:40:16.010 --> 00:40:17.170
The man's got a point.

00:40:17.730 --> 00:40:19.310
Anybody need a little refresher?

00:40:19.530 --> 00:40:21.470
Uh, just a couple fingers more there, Walt. Thanks.

00:40:21.650 --> 00:40:24.790
But you know, folks, as flawed as things are out there,

00:40:25.030 --> 00:40:28.210
I'd still stack them against any fantasy you can conjure.

00:40:28.450 --> 00:40:29.670
Well, thanks for...

00:40:29.670 --> 00:40:29.930
Yeah.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:33.040
outsider's perspective on humanity, Esau, but I think...

00:40:33.040 --> 00:40:34.660
What are your fantasies, Esau?

00:40:35.140 --> 00:40:36.900
Touring with the crash test dummies?

00:40:37.720 --> 00:40:38.980
Who needs fantasy?

00:40:39.510 --> 00:40:41.570
I've got the best of everything right here.

00:40:41.900 --> 00:40:44.860
I can have all the laughter and intellectual stimulation

00:40:44.860 --> 00:40:46.160
of an evening with friends,

00:40:46.380 --> 00:40:49.800
but I don't have to worry about heartburn or designated drivers

00:40:49.800 --> 00:40:51.740
or getting my feelings stepped on.

00:40:52.340 --> 00:40:53.760
I'd wish I were a real boy

00:40:53.760 --> 00:40:55.900
if it didn't mean growing up to be a real man.

00:40:59.530 --> 00:40:59.970
Well...

00:41:00.800 --> 00:41:03.100
You must be getting tired, Esau. Let's give it a rest, huh?

00:41:03.960 --> 00:41:05.500
He's a lot of fun, Chris.

00:41:06.240 --> 00:41:09.740
Yeah, but knocking aside, Ruthann, Esau makes some very good points.

00:41:09.880 --> 00:41:13.820
That whole western Ptolemaic view of the world with ego at its center.

00:41:14.130 --> 00:41:16.830
I think it's half the reason we've dug ourselves so deep, you know what I mean?

00:41:19.290 --> 00:41:22.270
Oh, look at the time. I'm gonna have to roll up my tent.

00:41:22.590 --> 00:41:24.150
Hey, hey, Ruthann, come on.

00:41:24.270 --> 00:41:27.640
We got a whole bottle of petite Syrah here. We hadn't even opened it yet.

00:41:39.400 --> 00:41:40.280
Get the wine, son.

00:41:40.480 --> 00:41:43.080
I want to talk about polystyrene versus pine

00:41:43.080 --> 00:41:44.280
in the dummy-making industry.

00:41:44.820 --> 00:41:45.220
Why?

00:41:45.500 --> 00:41:47.020
Is that a controversial issue?

00:41:47.520 --> 00:41:48.580
Genetic engineering?

00:41:49.140 --> 00:41:50.820
Don't get me started.

00:42:01.950 --> 00:42:05.700
Mr. Brewster, I'm glad I caught you.

00:42:06.470 --> 00:42:09.770
I'm disappointed, Shelley. I won't pretend I'm not.

00:42:10.960 --> 00:42:13.010
Here, I can't keep this.

00:42:16.460 --> 00:42:17.920
Yeah, I should have known.

00:42:18.340 --> 00:42:20.360
First apartheid goes, now this.

00:42:22.940 --> 00:42:24.650
I just wish I understood.

00:42:25.980 --> 00:42:27.620
Why'd you go after me? I'm nobody.

00:42:28.060 --> 00:42:29.560
Gee, I thought I explained that.

00:42:30.730 --> 00:42:32.070
You're my bread and butter, Shelly.

00:42:33.740 --> 00:42:37.060
Look, say I get some corporate raider to suck up some company,

00:42:37.240 --> 00:42:39.460
turn 3,000 employees out on the street.

00:42:39.750 --> 00:42:41.030
Where's the victory there?

00:42:41.880 --> 00:42:45.150
But if I can get somebody like you, pure of heart,

00:42:46.010 --> 00:42:51.030
to let her bumper stray over that white line just a little bit...

00:42:52.100 --> 00:42:57.580
You know that expression, God is in the details?

00:42:58.580 --> 00:42:59.060
Thanks, Seth.

00:43:00.600 --> 00:43:01.780
It's a little bit like that for me.

00:43:05.130 --> 00:43:06.050
What are you gonna do now?

00:43:07.610 --> 00:43:13.200
Well, there's a lady over in Nip Nip who's been clipping coupons out of her neighbor's newspaper.

00:43:13.640 --> 00:43:15.730
Not much, but it's a lead.

00:43:17.240 --> 00:43:19.980
Ah, I almost said good luck.

00:43:21.840 --> 00:43:22.980
I almost said thanks.

00:43:40.750 --> 00:43:42.030
Marilyn wasn't at her desk.

00:43:42.370 --> 00:43:42.690
What is it?

00:43:43.680 --> 00:43:45.820
I just thought maybe I could wash some windows.

00:43:46.040 --> 00:43:47.400
I noticed they were kind of dirty.

00:43:48.050 --> 00:43:49.050
Make up for what I did.

00:43:50.600 --> 00:43:50.920
Whatever.

00:43:54.920 --> 00:43:55.940
What are the mice for?

00:43:56.560 --> 00:43:59.820
Well, I'm thinking of following up on an experiment I started.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:03.220
in med school. Relatively straightforward question.

00:44:04.540 --> 00:44:07.460
What are the effects of induced hyponatremia on the basal palms?

00:44:07.740 --> 00:44:10.820
Which is salt depletion on the brain.

00:44:12.770 --> 00:44:13.690
Sounds important.

00:44:14.570 --> 00:44:15.850
Yeah, well, it isn't.

00:44:17.380 --> 00:44:20.780
See, the beauty of it is that these guys don't threaten thirst strikes.

00:44:21.040 --> 00:44:22.080
They don't trade medication.

00:44:22.320 --> 00:44:25.320
They definitely don't conspire to commit felony, so...

00:44:25.630 --> 00:44:27.600
The mice are good that way.

00:44:30.730 --> 00:44:32.270
I guess I'll get to the windows.

00:44:34.340 --> 00:44:34.660
Ed.

00:44:36.820 --> 00:44:37.700
Yeah, Dr. Fleischman.

00:44:39.110 --> 00:44:41.150
If you want, you can fill that water bottle up for me.

00:44:43.450 --> 00:44:43.690
All right.

00:44:54.150 --> 00:44:59.330
Chris Stevens flying solo once again, bidding a fond adieu to my good friend Esau.

00:45:00.160 --> 00:45:02.160
I hope the next wayward soul who finds him

00:45:02.160 --> 00:45:06.080
earns as much as I did from my brief but intense apprenticeship.

00:45:06.380 --> 00:45:12.040
It's funny, all the qualities that flow so naturally from Esau,

00:45:12.180 --> 00:45:15.520
like water from a spring melt are qualities that are in me.

00:45:16.480 --> 00:45:19.700
But embracing them means embracing Esau's black and white world

00:45:19.700 --> 00:45:22.020
and turning my back on the rainbow,

00:45:22.430 --> 00:45:25.330
letting one piercing note drum out the orchestra,

00:45:25.490 --> 00:45:29.650
one persuasive voice silence a clamoring chorus.

00:45:30.600 --> 00:45:31.580
I don't know, Esau.

00:45:31.840 --> 00:45:36.000
Maybe your corn-pone platitudes and straight-from-the-hip answers

00:45:36.000 --> 00:45:38.980
ring truer than my own fuzzy search for enlightenment.

00:45:39.460 --> 00:45:41.780
But sometimes we just need the uncertainty.

00:45:42.160 --> 00:45:44.340
I have to figure out exactly why.

00:45:44.670 --> 00:45:45.730
I may just look you up.

00:45:46.030 --> 00:45:48.450
Maybe then we'll have an act worth taking on the road, huh?
